also "hide" the second argument by                            ending the command line (first argument)                            with `` #'' (shell comment).          @@@@ccccoooollllttttooooaaaa(e)        Returns a string name for a column from                            the numeric argument.  For example:                                 @coltoa(@mycol-1)                                 @nval(coltoa(@mycol-1), @myrow+1)        BBBBuuuuiiiilllltttt----iiiinnnn FFFFiiiinnnnaaaannnncccciiiiaaaallll FFFFuuuunnnnccccttttiiiioooonnnnssss          Financial functions compute the mortgage (or loan) payment,          future value, and the present value functions.  Each accepts          three arguments, an amount, a rate of interest (per period),          and the number of periods.  These functions are the same as          those commonly found in other spreadsheets and financial          calculators          @@@@ppppmmmmtttt(e1,e2,e3)    @_p_m_t(60000,.01,360) computes the monthly                            payments for a $60000 mortgage at 12%                            annual interest (.01 per month) for 30                            years (360 months).          @@@@ffffvvvv(e1,e2,e3)     @_f_v(100,.005,36) computes the future value                            for of 36 monthly payments of $100 at 6%                            interest (.005 per month).  It answers the                            question: "How much will I have in 36                                  - 23 - Formatted:  November 18, 1991     SSSSCCCC((((1111))))                                                       SSSSCCCC((((1111))))                            months if I deposit $100 per month in a                            savings account paying 6% interest                            compounded monthly?"          @@@@ppppvvvv(e1,e2,e3)     @_p_v(1000,.015,36) computes the present                            value of an a ordinary annuity of 36                            monthly payments of $1000 at 18% annual                            interest.  It answers the question: "How                            much can I borrow at 18% for 30 years if I                            pay $1000 per month?"        BBBBuuuuiiiilllltttt----iiiinnnn DDDDaaaatttteeee aaaannnndddd TTTTiiiimmmmeeee FFFFuuuunnnnccccttttiiiioooonnnnssss          Time for _s_c follows the system standard: the number of          seconds since 1970.  All date and time functions except          @_d_a_t_e() return numbers, not strings.          @@@@nnnnoooowwww              Return the current time encoded as the                            number of seconds since the beginning of                            the epoch (December 31, 1969, midnight,                            GMT.)          @@@@ddddttttssss(e1,e2,e3)    @_d_t_s(9,14,1988) converts the date                            September 14, 1988 to the number of                            seconds from the epoch to the first second                            of 9/14/88, local time.  For example,                            @_d_a_t_e(@_d_t_s(12,14,1976)) yields                                 _T_u_e _D_e_c _1_4 _0_0:_0_0:_0_0 _1_9_7_6                            The month should be range from  1 to 12,                            the day should range from 1 to the number                            of days in the specified month, and the                            year should range from 1970 to 1999.          @@@@ttttttttssss(e1,e2,e3)    @_t_t_s(8,20,45) converts the time 8:40:45 to                            the number of seconds since midnight, the                            night before.  The hour should range from                            0 to 23; the minutes and seconds should                            range from 0 to 59.          The following functions take the time in seconds (e.g. from          @_n_o_w) as an argument and return the specified value.  The          functions all convert from GMT to local time.          @@@@ddddaaaatttteeee(e)          Convert the time in seconds to a date                            string 24 characters long in the following                            form:                                 _S_u_n _S_e_p _1_6 _0_1:_0_3:_5_2 _1_9_7_3                            Note that you can extract parts of this                            fixed-format string with @_s_u_b_s_t_r().                                  - 24 - Formatted:  November 18, 1991     SSSSCCCC((((1111))))                                                       SSSSCCCC((((1111))))          @@@@yyyyeeeeaaaarrrr(e)          Return the year.  Valid years begin with                            1970.  The last legal year is system                            dependent.          @@@@mmmmoooonnnntttthhhh(e)         Return the month, encoded as 1 (January)                            to 12 (December).          @@@@ddddaaaayyyy(e)           Return the day of the month, encoded as 1                            to 31.          @@@@hhhhoooouuuurrrr(e)          Return the number of hours since midnight,                            encoded as 0 to 23.          @@@@mmmmiiiinnnnuuuutttteeee(e)        Return the number of minutes since the                            last full hour, encoded as 0 to 59.          @@@@sssseeeeccccoooonnnndddd(e)        Return the number of seconds since the                            last full minute, encoded as 0